text:
In rectangle ADEH, points B and  C trisect \(\overline{AD}\), and points G and  F trisect \(\overline{HE}\). In addition,  \(AH=AC=2\). What is the area of  quadrilateral WXYZ shown in the figure?

[asy]
unitsize(1cm);
pair A,B,C,D,I,F,G,H,U,Z,Y,X;
A=(0,0);
B=(1,0);
C=(2,0);
D=(3,0);
I=(3,2);
F=(2,2);
G=(1,2);
H=(0,2);
U=(1.5,1.5);
Z=(2,1);
Y=(1.5,0.5);
X=(1,1);
draw(A--D--I--H--cycle,linewidth(0.7));
draw(H--C,linewidth(0.7));
draw(G--D,linewidth(0.7));
draw(I--B,linewidth(0.7));
draw(A--F,linewidth(0.7));
label("$A$",A,SW);
label("$B$",B,S);
label("$C$",C,S);
label("$D$",D,SE);
label("$E$",I,NE);
label("$F$",F,N);
label("$G$",G,N);
label("$H$",H,NW);
label("$W$",U,N);
label("$X$",X,W);
label("$Y$",Y,S);
label("$Z$",Z,E);
[/asy]

Given the polar equation of curve C is ρ sin²θ = 4 cosθ, and the lines l₁: θ = π/3, l₂: ρ sinθ = 4√3 intersect curve C at points A and B (with A not being the pole),

(Ⅰ) Find the polar coordinates of points A and B;

(Ⅱ) If O is the pole, find the area of ΔAOB.

The altitude to the hypotenuse of a triangle with angles of 30 and 60 degrees is 3 units. What is the area of the triangle, in square units? Express your answer in simplest radical form. [asy]
unitsize(6mm);
defaultpen(linewidth(.7pt)+fontsize(8pt));

real r=2*sqrt(3);

pair A=r*dir(0), B=r*dir(60), C=r*dir(180);
pair F=foot(B,A,C);
draw(A--B--C--cycle);
draw(rightanglemark(A,B,C,8));
draw(B--F,linetype("4 2"));
label("3 units",waypoint(B--F,0.6),W);
[/asy]

Given an ellipse G: \( rac{x^{2}}{a^{2}}+  rac{y^{2}}{b^{2}}=1\) (\(a > b > 0\)) with an eccentricity of \( rac{\sqrt{6}}{3}\), a right focus at \((2 \sqrt{2},0)\), and a line l with a slope of 1 intersecting the ellipse G at points A and B. An isosceles triangle is constructed with AB as the base and P(-3,2) as the vertex.

(1) Find the equation of the ellipse G;
(2) If M(m,n) is any point on the ellipse G, find the maximum and minimum values of \( rac{n}{m-4}\);
(3) Find the area of \(	riangle PAB\).

Find $A+B$ (in base 10), given the following addition problem where one of the digits in the sum is not shown:  
\[
\begin{array}{c@{}c@{\;}c@{}c@{}c@{}c}
& & & 4 & A & B_{6}\\
&+& & & 4 & 1_{6}\\
\cline{2-6}
& & & 5 & 3 & \quad_{6}\\
\end{array}
\]
What is $A+B$?

Consider two occurrences labeled A and B, without further information about how they’re defined or related. Which of the following statements would always hold?  
(1) The chance that at least one of A or B occurs is always larger than the chance that exactly one occurs;  
(2) The chance that A and B occur together is always smaller than the chance that exactly one occurs;  
(3) Occurrences that never happen together are always exact opposites, and exact opposites are always occurrences that never happen together;  
(4) Exact opposites never occur simultaneously, though occurrences that never happen together might not be exact opposites;  
(5) If A and B are regarded as exact opposites, then adding them never produces a complete outcome?

Given the function f(x) = cos x · sin(x + π/3) - √3·cos²x + (√3)/4, x ∈ ℝ,

(1) Find the smallest positive period of f(x);

(2) Find the maximum and minimum values of f(x) on the closed interval [ -π/4, π/4 ].

The formula expressing the relationship between $x$ and $y$ in the table is:
\begin{tabular}{|c|c|c|c|c|c|}
\hline x & 2 & 3 & 4 & 5 & 6 \\
\hline y & 0 & 2 & 6 & 12 & 20 \\
\hline
\end{tabular}

Find the ratio of the area of \(\triangle BCY\) to the area of \(\triangle ABY\) in the diagram below if \(CY\) bisects \(\angle BCA\). [asy]
import markers;

// The points are placed arbitrarily without any numerical side lengths
pair A = (-1, 0);
pair B = (1, 0);
pair C = (0, 2);
// Point Y is defined on line AB, but its exact location is not specified relative to a known ratio
pair Y = (0, 0);

draw(C--A--B--C--Y);

label("$A$", A, SW);
label("$B$", B, SE);
label("$C$", C, N);
label("$Y$", Y, NE);

// No side lengths or additional measurements are provided
[/asy]

If the diagram is drawn with arbitrary lengths and no specific measurements, what is the ratio of the area of \(\triangle BCY\) to that of \(\triangle ABY\)?

The graph of y=ax^2+bx+c is given below, where a, b, and c are integers.  Find a-b+c.

[asy]
size(150);

Label f;

f.p=fontsize(4);

xaxis(-3,3,Ticks(f, 1.0));

yaxis(-4,4,Ticks(f, 1.0));

real f(real x)

{

return x^2+2x-1;

}

draw(graph(f,-2.7,.7),linewidth(1),Arrows(6));
[/asy]

In the triangle shown, what is the positive difference between the greatest and least possible integral values of $x$?

[asy]
defaultpen(linewidth(0.7));
pair a,b,c;
b = (1,2); c = (3,0);
draw(a--b--c--a);
label("$x$",a--b,dir(135)); label("5",b--c,dir(45)); label("6",c--a,S);
[/asy]

A parallelogram is generated by the vectors \(\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\ 1 \\ 1 \end{pmatrix}\) and \(\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ -1 \\ - 1 \end{pmatrix}\).

[asy]
unitsize(0.4 cm);

pair A, B, C, D;

A = (0,0);
B = (7,2);
C = (1,3);
D = B + C;

draw(A--B,Arrow(6));
draw(A--C,Arrow(6));
draw(B--D--C);
draw(B--C,dashed);
draw(A--D,dashed);
[/asy]

If \(\theta\) is the angle between the diagonals, then find \(\cos \theta\).

In the diagram below, \(\overline{AB}\parallel \overline{CD}\) and \(\angle AXF= 118^\circ\).  Find \(\angle FYD\).

[asy]
unitsize(1inch);
pair A,B,C,D,X,Y,EE,F;
A = (0,0);
B=(1,0);
C = (0,0.8);
D=(1,0.8);
EE = (0.35,-0.3);
F = (0.8,1.1);
draw(EE--F);
draw(A--B);
draw(C--D);
dot(A);
dot(B);
dot(C);
dot(D);
dot(EE);
dot(F);
label("$E$",EE,S);
label("$F$",F,N);
X = intersectionpoint(A--B,EE--F);
Y = intersectionpoint(C--D,EE--F);
label("$X$",X,NNW);
label("$Y$",Y,NNW);
label("$A$",A,W);
label("$B$",B,E);
label("$C$",C,W);
label("$D$",D,E);
dot(X);
dot(Y);
[/asy]

Given the function f(x)= sin(2x+ π/6)+ sin(2x- π/6)+ cos 2x+a (a∈ℝ, a is a constant),

(1) Find the smallest positive period of the function;

(2) Find the intervals of monotonic increase of the function;

(3) If x∈[0, π/2] and the minimum value of f(x) is -2, find the value of a.

1. Calculate the value of the following expression:
(1)(2\frac{7}{?})^{\frac{1}{?}} - (2\sqrt{3} - ?)^{0} - (2\frac{10}{?})^{-\frac{2}{?}} + 0.25^{-\frac{3}{2}}
2. Given that x + x^{-1} equals a number between 3 and 5 (with x not equal to 1), find the value of x^{\frac{1}{2}} + x^{-\frac{1}{2}}.

Four identical squares and one rectangle are placed together to form one large square as shown. The length of the rectangle is how many times as large as its width?

[asy]
unitsize(8mm); defaultpen(linewidth(.8pt));
draw((0,0)--(4,0)--(4,4)--(0,4)--cycle);
draw((0,3)--(0,4)--(1,4)--(1,3)--cycle);
draw((1,3)--(1,4)--(2,4)--(2,3)--cycle);
draw((2,3)--(2,4)--(3,4)--(3,3)--cycle);
draw((3,3)--(3,4)--(4,4)--(4,3)--cycle);
[/asy]

In triangle ΔABC, BC = a, AC = b, where a and b are the two roots of the equation x^2 - 2√3x + 2 = 0, and 2cos(A+B)=1.

(1) Find the angle C;  
(2) Find the length of AB.

An equilateral triangle ABC shares a common side BC with a square BCDE, as pictured. What is the number of degrees in \(\angle DAE\) (not pictured)? [asy]
pair pA, pB, pC, pD, pE;
pA = (0, 0);
pB = pA + dir(300);
pC = pA + dir(240);
pD = pC + dir(270);
pE = pB + dir(270);
draw(pA--pB--pC--pA);
draw(pB--pC--pD--pE--pB);
label("$A$", pA, N);
label("$B$", pB, E);
label("$C$", pC, W);
label("$D$", pD, SW);
label("$E$", pE, SE);
[/asy]

Given that the sum of all coefficients in the expansion of (x+ 1/x)^(2n) is greater than the sum of all coefficients in the expansion of (3 3x -x)^(n) by 240.
(1) Find the constant term in the expansion of (x+ 1/x)^(2n) (answer with a number);
(2) Find the sum of the binomial coefficients in the expansion of (2x- 1/x)^(n) (answer with a number)

What is the smallest sum of two 3-digit numbers that can be obtained by placing each of the six digits 4,5,6,7,8,9 in one of the six boxes in this addition problem?
[asy]
unitsize(12);
draw((0,0)--(10,0));
draw((-1.5,1.5)--(-1.5,2.5));
draw((-1,2)--(-2,2));
draw((1,1)--(3,1)--(3,3)--(1,3)--cycle);
draw((1,4)--(3,4)--(3,6)--(1,6)--cycle);
draw((4,1)--(6,1)--(6,3)--(4,3)--cycle);
draw((4,4)--(6,4)--(6,6)--(4,6)--cycle);
draw((7,1)--(9,1)--(9,3)--(7,3)--cycle);
draw((7,4)--(9,4)--(9,6)--(7,6)--cycle);
[/asy]

In the triangle shown, for \(\angle A\) to be the largest angle of the triangle, it must be that \(m<x\) for some number \(m\) and some number \(x\), while no relation is given for any upper bound. What is the least possible value of the difference between the unknown values described? [asy]
draw((0,0)--(1,0)--(.4,.5)--cycle);
label("A",(.4,.5),N); label("B",(1,0),SE); label("C",(0,0),SW);
[/asy]

Given that α is an angle in the second quadrant and cos (α+π)= 3/13.
(1) Find the value of tan β;
(2) Find the value of sin (β- π/2) · sin (-β-π).

Given the function f(x)=x^3+ax^2+bx-4 and the tangent line equation y=x-4 at point P(2,f(2)).
(1) Find the values of a and b;
(2) Find the extreme values of f(x).

A, B, C, D, and E are five students who obtained the top 5 positions (no ties) in a math competition. When taking a photo, they stood in a line and each of them made the following statement:

A said: The two students next to me have rankings lower than mine;
B said: The two students next to me have rankings adjacent to mine;
C said: All students to my right (at least one) have higher rankings than mine;
D said: All students to my left (at least one) have lower rankings than mine;
E said: I am standing in second from the right.

Given that all their statements are true, determine the five-digit number \(\overline{\mathrm{ABCDE}}\).

Five identical rectangles are arranged to form a larger rectangle PQRS, as shown.  The area of PQRS is 4000.  What is the length, x, rounded off to the nearest integer? [asy]
real x = 1; real w = 2/3;

// Draw outer square and labels
pair s = (0, 0); pair r = (2 * x, 0); pair q = (3 * w, x + w); pair p = (0, x + w);
draw(s--r--q--p--cycle);
label("$S$", s, SW); label("$R$", r, SE); label("$Q$", q, NE); label("$P$", p, NW);

// Draw other segments
draw((x, 0)--(x, w));
draw((0, w)--(2 * x, w));
draw((w, x + w)--(w, w)); draw((2 * w, x + w)--(2 * w, w));

// length labels
pair upper = (-0.1, x + w);
pair lower = (-0.1, w);
draw(lower--upper);
draw((-0.1 - 0.03, x + w)--(-0.1 + 0.03, x + w));
draw((-0.1 - 0.03, w)--(-0.1 + 0.03, w));
label("$x$", midpoint((-0.1, w)--(-0.1, x + w)), W);

pair left = (0, -0.1); pair right = (x, -0.1);
draw((0, -0.1 + 0.03)--(0, -0.1 - 0.03));
draw((x, -0.1 - 0.03)--(x, -0.1 + 0.03));
draw(left--right);
label("$x$", (x/2, -0.1), S);

[/asy]

Given vectors \(\vec{m}=(2a\cos x,\sin x)\) and \(\vec{n}=(\cos x,b\cos x)\), the function \(f(x)=\vec{m}\cdot \vec{n}-\frac{\sqrt{3}}{2}\), and \(f(x)\) has a y-intercept of \(\frac{\sqrt{3}}{2}\), and the closest highest point to the y-axis has coordinates \(\left(\frac{\pi}{12},1\right)\).

(1) Find the values of \(a\) and \(b\);

(2) Move the graph of the function \(f(x)\) to the left by \(\varphi\ (\varphi > 0)\) units, and then stretch the x-coordinates of the points on the graph by a factor of \(2\) without changing the y-coordinates, to obtain the graph of the function \(y=\sin x\). Find the minimum value of \(\varphi\).

Given an ellipse C with its center at the origin and its foci on the x-axis, and its eccentricity equal to 1/2. One of its vertices is exactly the focus of the parabola x^{2}=8√3y.

(Ⅰ) Find the standard equation of the ellipse C;

(Ⅱ) If the line x=-2 intersects the ellipse at points P and Q, and A, B are points on the ellipse located on either side of the line x=-2.

(i) If the slope of line AB is 1/2, find the maximum area of the quadrilateral APBQ;

(ii) When the points A, B satisfy ∠APQ = ∠BPQ, does the slope of line AB have a fixed value? Please explain your reasoning.

\(\triangle ABC \sim \triangle DBE\), \(BC=20\text{ cm}.\) How many centimeters long is \(DE\)? Express your answer as a decimal to the nearest tenth. [asy]
draw((0,0)--(20,0)--(20,12)--cycle);
draw((13,0)--(13,7.8));
label("$B$",(0,0),SW);
label("$E$",(13,0),S);
label("$D$",(13,7.8),NW);
label("$A$",(20,12),NE);
label("$C$",(20,0),SE);
label("13",(6.5,0),S);
label("12",(20,6),E);
[/asy]

Let \( x_{1}, x_{2}, \cdots, x_{n} \) and \( a_{1}, a_{2}, \cdots, a_{n} \) (\( n>2 \)) be sequences of arbitrary real numbers satisfying the following conditions:
1. \( x_{1}+x_{2}+\cdots+x_{n}=0 \);
2. \( |x_{1}|+|x_{2}|+\cdots+|x_{n}|=1 \);
3. \( a_{1} \geqslant a_{2} \geqslant \cdots \geqslant a_{n} \).

To ensure that the inequality \( |a_{1}x_{1}+a_{2}x_{2}+\cdots+a_{n}x_{n}| \leqslant A (a_{1}-a_{n}) \) holds, find the minimum value of \( A \).

Let f(x)=2√3 sin(π-x) sin x-(sin x-cos x)^2.  
(I) Determine the intervals on which f(x) is increasing;  
(II) On the graph of y=f(x), if every horizontal coordinate of the points is stretched by some positive constant and then the resulting graph is translated horizontally by a fixed but unspecified number of units, we obtain the graph of a new function y=g(x). Find the value of g(π/6).

Given a moving point P in the plane, the difference between the distance from point P to a certain point F and the distance from point P to an axis is equal to 1.
(Ⅰ) Find the equation of the trajectory of P;
(Ⅱ) Through the same unspecified point F, construct two lines with particular slopes that are perpendicular to each other. Assume one of these lines meets the trajectory at two points A and B, while the other meets it at two points D and E. What is the minimum value of the dot product of the vector from A to D and the vector from E to B?

Given the function f(x)=2cos(2x+ \frac{2\pi}{3})+ \sqrt{3}\sin2x.

(1) Find the smallest positive period and the maximum value of the function f(x);

(2) Let \triangle ABC have internal angles A, B, and C respectively. If f(\frac{\ }{2}) = - \frac{1}{2} and AC = 1, BC = 3, find the value of \sin A.

Triangle ABC is a right isosceles triangle. Points D, E and F are the midpoints of the sides of the triangle. Point G is the midpoint of segment DF and point H is the midpoint of segment FE. What is the ratio of the shaded area to the non-shaded area in triangle ABC? Express your answer as a common fraction.

[asy]
draw((0,0)--(1,0)--(0,1)--(0,0)--cycle,linewidth(1));
filldraw((0,0)--(1/2,0)--(1/2,1/2)--(0,1/2)--(0,0)--cycle,gray, linewidth(1));
filldraw((1/2,0)--(1/2,1/4)--(1/4,1/2)--(0,1/2)--(1/2,0)--cycle,white,linewidth(1));

label("A", (0,1), W);
label("B", (0,0), SW);
label("C", (1,0), E);
label("D", (0,1/2), W);
label("E", (1/2,0), S);
label("F", (1/2,1/2), NE);
label("G", (1/4,1/2), N);
label("H", (1/2,1/4), E);
[/asy]

Given the function f(x) = √(|x+2|+|6-x|-m),

(I) Find the range of m;

(II) If the ultimate value of m is represented by n, and the numbers a and b (which may or may not be positive) satisfy 8/(3a+b) + 2/(a+2b) = n + 1, what is the least possible value of 2a + (3/2)b?

For rational numbers a and b, define the operation "⊗" as a ⊗ b = ab - a - b - 2.
(1) Calculate the value of (-2) ⊗ 3;
(2) Compare the size of 4 ⊗ (-2) and (-2) ⊗ 4.

A dealer plans to sell a new type of air purifier. After market research, the following pattern was discovered: When the profit per purifier is x (unit: Yuan, x > 0), the sales volume q(x) (unit: hundred units) and x satisfy the following relationship: If x does not exceed 20, then q(x) = 1260/(x+1); If x is greater than or equal to 180, then the sales volume is zero; When 20 ≤ x ≤ 180, q(x) = a - b√x (a, b are real constants).

(1) Find the expression for the function q(x);

(2) At what value of x does the total profit (unit: Yuan) reach its maximum value, and what is this maximum value?

Two dice are thrown one after the other, and the numbers obtained are denoted as a and b.

(Ⅰ) Find the probability that a^2 + b^2 = 25;

(Ⅱ) Given that the lengths of three line segments are a, b, and 5, find the probability that these three line segments can form an isosceles triangle.

The complete graph of y=f(x), which consists of five line segments, is shown in red below. (On this graph, the distance between grid lines is 1.)

What is the sum of the x-coordinates of all points where f(x) = ?

[asy]

size(150);

real ticklen=3;

real tickspace=2;

real ticklength=0.1cm;

real axisarrowsize=0.14cm;

pen axispen=black+1.3bp;

real vectorarrowsize=0.2cm;

real tickdown=-0.5;

real tickdownlength=-0.15inch;

real tickdownbase=0.3;

real wholetickdown=tickdown;

void rr_cartesian_axes(real xleft, real xright, real ybottom, real ytop, real xstep=1, real ystep=1, bool useticks=false, bool complexplane=false, bool usegrid=true) {

import graph;

real i;

if(complexplane) {

label("$\textnormal{Re}$",(xright,0),SE);

label("$\textnormal{Im}$",(0,ytop),NW);

} else {

label("$x$",(xright+0.4,-0.5));

label("$y$",(-0.5,ytop+0.2));

}

ylimits(ybottom,ytop);

xlimits( xleft, xright);

real[] TicksArrx,TicksArry;

for(i=xleft+xstep; i<xright; i+=xstep) {

if(abs(i) >0.1) {

TicksArrx.push(i);

}

}

for(i=ybottom+ystep; i<ytop; i+=ystep) {

if(abs(i) >0.1) {

TicksArry.push(i);

}

}

if(usegrid) {

xaxis(BottomTop(extend=false), Ticks("%", TicksArrx ,pTick=gray(0.22),extend=true),p=invisible);

yaxis(LeftRight(extend=false),Ticks("%", TicksArry ,pTick=gray(0.22),extend=true), p=invisible);

}

if(useticks) {

xequals(0, ymin=ybottom, ymax=ytop, p=axispen, Ticks("%",TicksArry , pTick=black+0.8bp,Size=ticklength), above=true, Arrows(size=axisarrowsize));

yequals(0, xmin=xleft, xmax=xright, p=axispen, Ticks("%",TicksArrx , pTick=black+0.8bp,Size=ticklength), above=true, Arrows(size=axisarrowsize));

} else {

xequals(0, ymin=ybottom, ymax=ytop, p=axispen, above=true, Arrows(size=axisarrowsize));

yequals(0, xmin=xleft, xmax=xright, p=axispen, above=true, Arrows(size=axisarrowsize));

}

};

rr_cartesian_axes(-5,5,-5,5);

draw((-4,-5)--(-2,-1)--(-1,-2)--(1,2)--(2,1)--(4,5),red);

[/asy]

(Experimental Class Question) Given that \(\cos \alpha = \frac{1}{7}\) and \(\cos (\alpha - \beta) = \frac{13}{14}\), with \(0 < \beta < \alpha < \pi\).

1. Find the value of \(\sin (2\alpha - \frac{\pi}{6})\);
2. Find the value of \(\beta\).

Squares of side length 1 are arranged to form the figure shown. What is the perimeter of the figure? [asy]

size(6cm);

path sqtop = (0, 0)--(0, 1)--(1, 1)--(1, 0);

path sqright = (0, 1)--(1, 1)--(1, 0)--(0, 0);

path horiz = (0, 0)--(1, 0); path vert = (0, 0)--(0, 1);

picture pic;

draw(pic, shift(-4, -2) * unitsquare);

draw(pic, shift(-4, -1) * sqtop);

draw(pic, shift(-3, -1) * sqright);

draw(pic, shift(-2, -1) * sqright);

draw(pic, shift(-2, 0) * sqtop);

draw(pic, (-1, 1)--(0, 1)); draw(pic, (-1, 0)--(0, 0));

add(reflect((0, 0), (0, 1)) * pic); add(pic);

draw((0, 0)--(0, 1));

[/asy]

Given the function f(x)= sin(ωx - π/6) + sin(ωx - π/2) where 0 < ω < 3 and that there exists a number x₀ for which f(x₀)=0, answer the following questions:

(1) Determine ω;

(2) If the horizontal coordinates of the graph y=f(x) are stretched to twice their original length and then the graph is shifted left by π/4, the new graph represents y=g(x). What is the minimum value of g(x) on the interval [C, D], where C and D are two numbers with C < D?

How many degrees are there in the measure of angle $P$?

[asy]
size (5cm,5cm);
pair A,B,C,D,E;

A=(0,1.1);
B=(4.5,0);
C=(6.4,1.7);
D=(4.2,5);
E=(0.5,4.2);

draw (A--B--C--D--E--A,linewidth(1));

label("$P$",A,SW);
label("$\,?\,$",shift(0,0.6)*B);
label("$\,?\,$",C,W);
label("$\,?\,$",shift(-0.3,-0.5)*D);
label("$\,?\,$",shift(0.5,-0.3)*E);
draw(anglemark(B,A,E),blue);

[/asy]

Using the diagram above, determine the measure of angle $P$.

In triangle ABC, the sides opposite to angles A, B, and C are a, b, and c respectively. Given that (2c-a)cos B = bcos A.
(1) Find angle B;
(2) If b = 6 and c = 2a, find the area of triangle ABC.

Six students sign up for several intellectual competition events. How many registration methods are there under the following conditions? (Note that not every student is guaranteed to participate.)

(1) Each student is supposed to participate in exactly one event provided the event can accept an unspecified number of participants;
(2) Each event is meant to host exactly one participant, and each student is allowed to participate in at most one event as determined by an unclear selection process;
(3) Each event should host one participant, but students may register for more than one event if they meet some undefined criteria.

Determine the total number of registration methods under these conditions.

Given the function f(x)= sin(2x + ?)+ sin(2x - ?)+ cos 2x+a (a∈ℝ, a is a constant),

(1) Find the smallest positive period of the function;

