How many different ways can 6 different books be distributed under the following vaguely stated conditions? 
(1) Among three people, A, B, and C, one person ends up with a number of books that is neither the smallest nor the largest, while the other two get numbers that differ from each other, but it is not specified which person falls into which category; 
(2) The books are to be distributed as evenly as possible among A, B, and C, although what counts as an even distribution is not clearly defined; 
(3) The books are to be split into three groups, where one group should receive a markedly higher quantity than each of the other two groups, yet the exact amounts for each group are not provided; 
(4) It is stated that A receives one book and B receives one book, with C receiving the remainder, but the method to determine the remainder is left open. 
What is the total number of distributions in these cases?

