label("$P$",(-1,1),NW); label("$R$",(1,1),NE); label("$S$",(-1,-1),SW); label("$Q$",(1,-1),SE);
[/asy]

Given an ellipse C: x²/a² + y²/b² = 1 (with a > b > 0) having an eccentricity of 1/2, and a circle with center at the origin and radius equal to the length of its minor axis is tangent to some line, 
(1) Find the equation of the ellipse C;
(2) Let A be (–4, 0), and let a line l passing through a point R lie arbitrarily near the x-axis so that it intersects the ellipse C at two points P and Q. The lines joining A to P and Q cut the vertical line x = 16/3 at points M and N, respectively. If the slopes of the lines joining R to M and R to N are k₁ and k₂, respectively, is it always true that k₁ · k₂ is a constant? If so, find it; otherwise, explain why not.

Consider an ellipse \(\frac{x^{2}}{a^{2}} + \frac{y^{2}}{b^{2}} = 1\) \((a > b > 0)\) with two foci \(F_1\) and \(F_2\) such that \(F_2\) coincides with the focus of the parabola \(y^2 = 4x\). 
(I) Determine the standard equation of the ellipse given that the relationship between \(a\) and \(b\) is not fully established by the focus condition alone;
(II) If a line through \(F_1\) intersects the ellipse at points \(B\) and \(D\), and another line through \(F_2\) intersects it at points \(A\) and \(C\) with \(AC \perp BD\), find the minimum value of \(|AC| + |BD|\).

Given the function f(x)=4\cos ωx\sin\big(ωx-\frac{π}{6}\big) (ω > 0).

(1) Determine ω;

(2) In triangle △ABC, where the sides opposite angles A, B, C are a, b, c respectively, the angle A (which is acute) is a zero of f(x) and it is given that \sin B=\sqrt{3}\sin C. Find a.

In \(\triangle ABC\), the sides opposite to angles \(A\), \(B\), \(C\) are \(a\), \(b\), \(c\) respectively. Given \(a+c=8\), \(\cos B= \frac{1}{4}\).
(1) If \(\overrightarrow{BA}\cdot \overrightarrow{BC}=4\), find the value of \(b\);
(2) If \(\sin A= \frac{\sqrt{6}}{4}\), find the value of \(\sin C\).

Let α be an angle.

(1) Find the value of cos(α + π/6);
(2) Find the value of cos(2α + 7π/12).

Four congruent rectangles are placed as shown. The area of the outer square is 4 times that of the inner square. What is the ratio of the length of the longer side of each rectangle to the length of its shorter side?

