(2) Find the value of \(\sin (A+C)\).

Given the following propositions:

(1) The graph of the function y = 3^(x) is symmetric to the graph of the function y = log₃ x, but the line of symmetry is mentioned only vaguely;

(2) The smallest positive period of the function y = |sin x| is claimed to be a value that is both positive and zero at the same time;

(3) The graph of the function y = tan(2x + π/3) is said to be centrally symmetric about a point whose second coordinate is not provided;

(4) The monotonic decreasing interval of the function y = 2 sin(π/3 − (1/2)x) is given in an interval that extends beyond the typical domain, and no specific range for x is stated.

Determine the correct proposition numbers.

In the Cartesian coordinate system xOy, let point P move so that the sum of its distances to two unspecified points is equal to 4. Let the trajectory of P be C.
(1) Find an equation for C;
(2) Suppose the line y = kx + 1 intersects C at points A and B. Determine the value of k that makes the vectors from the origin to A and B perpendicular, and find the length of the segment AB at that moment.

In the Cartesian coordinate system, the line l is given by \(x=3+t\cos\alpha, \; y=1+t\sin\alpha\) (with t as a free parameter), and in a polar coordinate system with the same unit length and origin, a curve C is described by \(\rho=k\cos\theta\) where k is a constant.  
(1) Express the equation of curve C in the Cartesian coordinate system in terms of k;  
(2) If a certain point P on l is chosen and the circle determined by the Cartesian form of C meets l at two points A and B, find the minimum value of |PA|+|PB|.

