Given a sequence \(\{a_n\}\), the sum of the first \(n\) terms \(S_n\) satisfies \(a_{n+1}=2S_n+6\), and \(a_1=6\).

(Ⅰ) Find the general formula for the sequence \(\{a_n\}\);

(Ⅱ) Let \(b_n=\frac{a_n}{(a_n-2)(a_{n+1}-2)}\), and \(T_n\) be the sum of the first \(n\) terms of the sequence \(\{b_n\}\). Is there a maximum integer \(m\) such that for any \(n\in \mathbb{N}^*\), \(T_n > \frac{m}{16}\) holds? If it exists, find \(m\); if not, explain why.

