Let the function be \(f(x)=\sin(2\omega x+\frac{\pi}{3})+\frac{\sqrt{3}}{2}+a\) with \(\omega>0\). It is given that the graph of \(f(x)\) has its first highest point somewhere to the right of the y-axis. 

(1) Determine the value of \(\omega\);  
(2) Assuming that on an unspecified interval the minimum value of \(f(x)\) is \(\sqrt{3}\), find the value of \(a\);  
(3) If \(g(x)=f(x)-a\), describe in detail how the graph of \(y=\sin x\) is transformed to obtain the graph of \(g(x)\). In your description, include the axis of symmetry and the center of symmetry for \(g(x)\).

Note: The location of the first highest point is not precisely specified, and the interval for the minimum value is not given explicitly.

Triangle ABC is an isosceles triangle with AB=BC. Point D is the midpoint of both \(\overline{BC}\) and \(\overline{AE}\), and \(\overline{CE}\) is 11 units long. What is the length of \(\overline{BD}\)?  Express your answer as a decimal to the nearest tenth.

