(1) find angles A and C;
(2) find the area of \( \triangle ABC \).

Given the sets
$$
\begin{array}{l}
A=\{(x, y) \mid |x| + |y| = a, a > 0\}, \\
B=\{(x, y) \mid |xy| + 1 = |x| + |y|\}
\end{array}
$$

If A \cap B forms the vertices of a regular octagon in the plane, find the value of a.

Given the function f(x)= sin(ωx)cos(ωx) - √3cos²(ωx) + (√3)/2 (ω > 0) and noting that its graph is stated to have a pair of symmetry lines whose separation is not clearly defined, 

(1) Determine the equation of one of its symmetry lines assuming it goes through an unspecified point; 

(2) If the zeros of y=f(x)-1/3 on some open interval are labeled x₁ and x₂, what is cos(x₁-x₂)?

Given that f(α) = (sqrt((1 - sin α)/(1 + sin α)) + sqrt((1 + sin α)/(1 - sin α)))cos³α + 2sin(π/2 + α)cos(3π/2 + α) (where α is an angle in the third quadrant),
(I) find the value of f(α) when tan α = 2;
(II) find the value of tan α when f(α) = (2/5)cos α.

Given f(x) = 4cos x sin(x + π/6) - 1.
(Ⅰ) Determine the smallest positive period of f(x);
(Ⅱ) Find the maximum and minimum values of f(x) in the interval [-π/6, π/4].

Given the parabola C: x² = 2py (p > 0) with focus F, and the minimum distance between F and a point on the circle M: x² + (y+4)² = 1 is 4.
(1) Find p;
(2) If point P lies on M, PA and PB are two tangents to C with points A and B as the points of tangency, find the maximum area of △PAB.

In \(\triangle ABC\), \(2\sin 2C\cdot\cos C-\sin 3C= \sqrt {3}(1-\cos C)\).
(1) Find the measure of angle \(C\);
(2) If \(AB=2\), and \(\sin C+\sin (B-A)=2\sin 2A\), find the area of \(\triangle ABC\).

In the Cartesian coordinate system xOy, there is a circle C₁: (x-2)²+(y-4)²=20. With the origin O as the pole and the positive half-axis of x as the polar axis, a polar coordinate system is established. For C₂: θ = π/3 (ρ ∈ ℝ).

(1) Find the polar equation of C₁ and the Cartesian coordinate equation of C₂;  
(2) If the polar equation of line C₃ is θ = π/6 (ρ ∈ ℝ), and assuming the intersection points of C₂ and C₁ are O and M, and the intersection points of C₃ and C₁ are O and N, find the area of ΔOMN.

Given the parametric equation of line l as \(\begin{cases} x = m + \frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}t \\ y = \frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}t \end{cases}\) (with t as the parameter), establish a polar coordinate system with the origin as the pole and the positive x-axis as the polar axis. The polar equation of curve C is given by \(\rho^{2}\cos^{2}\theta+3\rho^{2}\sin^{2}\theta = c\), where c is a constant whose value is not specified, and the left focus F of the curve C lies on line l.

(1) If line l intersects curve C at points A and B, determine the value of |FA| \cdot |FB|;

(2) Find the maximum value of the perimeter of the inscribed rectangle of curve C.

Chinese mathematician Hua Luogeng saw a brain teaser in a magazine that a passenger was reading during a trip abroad: find the cube root of 59319. Hua Luogeng immediately provided an answer that stunned everyone. When asked to explain the secret of his calculation method, Xiaochao began an exploration process which now has several unclear steps. Please complete the process below:

(1) Find the cube root of 59319.
① By comparing two unconnected perfect cubes, it is suggested that the cube root of 59319 has ____ digits;
② Noting the final digit of 59319 and invoking a nonstandard rule on cube endings, deduce that the cube root of 59319 ends in ____;
③ If one subtracts an arbitrarily chosen section of the number and then uses an inconsistent set of cube comparisons, what then is the complete cube root of 59319?

(2) Given that 195112 is also described as the cube of an integer, and using a similar method that involves subtracting an unspecified group of digits and comparing with unrelated cubes, determine the cube root of 195112.

Given the function f(x) = 1/3 x^3 - 4x + 4, 
(I) Find the extreme values of the function;
(II) Find the maximum and minimum values of the function on the interval [-3, 4].

Let f be defined by  \[f(x) = \left\{
\begin{array}{cl}
2-x & \text{ if } x \leq 1, \\
2x-x^2 & \text{ if } x>1.
\end{array}
\right.\]Calculate f^{-1}(-3)+f^{-1}(0)+f^{-1}(3).

On an indoor circular track of circumference 50 feet, Joneal starts at point S, runs in a counterclockwise direction, and then stops when he has run exactly one mile (5280 feet). On which quarter of the circle, A, B, C or D, did Joneal stop?

[asy]
import graph;
draw(Circle((0,0),20));
draw((-25,0)--(25,0));
draw((0,-25)--(0,25));
dot((20,0));
label("S",(20,0),SE);
label("A",(17,17),E);
label("B",(-17,17),W);
label("C",(-17,-17),W);
label("D",(17,-17),E);
[/asy]

(The full score of this question is 14 points) It is known that A and B are two fixed points on a plane, and the moving point P satisfies |PA| + |PB| = 2.
(1) Find the equation of the trajectory of point P;
(2) Suppose the line l: y = k (k > 0) intersects the trajectory of point P from (1) at points M and N, find the maximum area of △BMN and the equation of line l at this time.

Find all real numbers $x,y,z$ so that
\begin{align*}
    x^2 y + y^2 z + z^2 &= 0 \\
    z^3 + z^2 y + z y^3 + x^2 y &= \frac{1}{4}(x^4 + y^4).
\end{align*}

The shaded region shown consists of 11 unit squares and rests along the x-axis and the y-axis. The shaded region is rotated about the x-axis to form a solid. In cubic units, what is the volume of the resulting solid? Express your answer in simplest form in terms of π.

[asy]
defaultpen(linewidth(0.7));
fill((0,0)--(0,5)--(1,5)--(1,2)--(4,2)--(4,0)--cycle, gray(.8));
draw((0,0)--(0,5)--(1,5)--(1,2)--(4,2)--(4,0)--cycle);
draw((0,1)--(4,1));
draw((0,2)--(1,2));
draw((0,3)--(1,3));
draw((0,4)--(1,4));
draw((1,0)--(1,2));
draw((2,0)--(2,2));
draw((3,0)--(3,2));

draw((4,0)--(5,0),EndArrow(4));
draw((0,0)--(-2,0),EndArrow(4));
label("$x$", (5,0), E);

draw((0,5)--(0,6),EndArrow(4));
draw((0,0)--(0,-1),EndArrow(4));
label("$y$", (0,6), N);
[/asy]

In the polar coordinate system, circle C is centered at point C(2, -π/6) with a radius of 2.

(1) Find the polar equation of circle C;

(2) Find the length of the chord cut from circle C by the line l: θ = -5π/12 (ρ ∈ ℝ).

Two circles of radius r are externally tangent to each other and internally tangent to the ellipse x^2 + 5y^2 = 6, as shown below. Find r.
[asy]
size(7cm);
draw(scale(sqrt(6), sqrt(6)/sqrt(5))* unitcircle);
draw((0,-1.5)--(0,1.7),EndArrow);
draw((-3,0)--(3,0),EndArrow);
draw(Circle( (sqrt(0.96),0), sqrt(0.96) ));
draw(Circle( (-sqrt(0.96),0), sqrt(0.96) ));
label("$x$",(3,0),E);label("$y$",(0,1.7),N);
[/asy]

In \(\triangle ABC\), \(a\), \(b\), \(c\) are the sides opposite to angles \(A\), \(B\), \(C\) respectively. Given that \(a^{2}-c^{2}=b^{2}- \frac {8bc}{5}\) and \(\sin B= \frac {4}{5}\),

(I) Find the value of \(\sin A\);

(II) Find the area of \(\triangle ABC\).

Given the function f(x) = 1/3 x^3 - 4x + 4,
(I) Find the extreme values of the function;
(II) Find the maximum and minimum values of the function on the interval [a, b].

Triangle ABC has its vertices A, B, and C on the sides of a rectangle 4 units by 5 units as shown. What is the area of triangle ABC in square units?

[asy]

fill((0,1)--(4,0)--(2,5)--cycle,lightgray);

for(int i=1; i < 5; ++i){
  for(int k=1; k < 4; ++k){
    draw((0,i)--(4,i),dashed);
    draw((k,0)--(k,5),dashed);
  }
}

draw((0,0)--(4,0)--(4,5)--(0,5)--(0,0));

draw((0,1)--(4,0)--(2,5)--(0,1));

label("$A$",(0,1),W);
label("$B$",(4,0),SE);
label("$C$",(2,5),N);

[/asy]

In △ABC, the sides opposite to angles A, B, C are a, b, c, respectively. Given sinAcosB - (2c - cosA)•sinB = 0.
(1) Find the value of b;
(2) Find the maximum value of the perimeter of △ABC.

In \(\triangle ABC\), \(2\sin2C\cdot\cos C - \sin3C = \sqrt{3}(1-\cos A)\).
(1) Find the measure of an angle in \(\triangle ABC\);
(2) If \(AB = 2\) and \(\sin C + \sin(B-A) = 2\sin2A\), determine the area of \(\triangle ABC\).

